  product_analysis: target_segments=['older adults managing multiple daily prescriptions', 'elderly users with memory or cognitive decline', 'seniors living alone without regular caregiver support', 'older adults with complex medication schedules and refill needs', 'caregivers helping an elderly family member manage meds'] product_summary='A mobile app designed to help elderly users manage medications by simplifying daily adherence, reminders, refill tracking, and medication information. The app should support different levels of independence, from seniors who want a simple personal pill reminder to those with memory challenges who need more guided prompts, as well as caregivers who may help monitor adherence remotely. Key value includes reducing missed doses, lowering confusion about what to take and when, and easing the burden of keeping track of multiple prescriptions, schedules, and refills.'
  personas: [{'_map_index': 0, 'name': 'Margaret Ellis', 'segment': 'older adults managing multiple daily prescriptions', 'profile': '# Margaret Ellis\n\n## Demographics\n- **Age:** 76\n- **Location:** Richmond, Virginia, USA\n- **Occupation:** Retired middle school librarian\n\n## Goals\n1. **Keep her medication schedule straight without relying on memory.** Margaret takes seven prescriptions at different times of day and wants a simple way to see what to take, when to take it, and whether she already took it.\n2. **Avoid accidental double-dosing or missed doses.** She wants the app to clearly confirm each dose so she doesn’t second-guess herself later in the day.\n3. **Stay on top of refill timing and prescription changes.** Margaret wants reminders when a bottle is running low and an easy way to update the app after her doctor changes a dose.\n\n## Frustrations\n1. **Pill bottles all look alike and the labels are hard to read.** She often has to put on her reading glasses and still struggles to find the right medication name or dosage quickly.\n2. **Current reminder methods feel too easy to ignore or too vague.** Sticky notes and phone alarms don’t tell her *which* pill to take, and she sometimes dismisses notifications without acting on them.\n3. **She worries about making a mistake when routines change.** If a prescription is temporarily paused, moved to a different time, or replaced at the pharmacy, she finds it stressful to update her system and remember the new routine.\n\n## Context\nMargaret is moderately comfortable with technology. She uses a smartphone for calls, text messages, weather, and photos of her grandchildren, but she prefers apps with large text, clear buttons, and very few steps. She checks her medications in the kitchen each morning after breakfast and again at lunch and bedtime. Her apartment is quiet, well-lit, and organized, but she keeps her pill organizer near the coffee maker so she can’t forget it. She often takes medications while watching the news, so she needs reminders that are noticeable without being overwhelming.\n\n## Key quote\n"I don’t need anything fancy — I just need to know, in plain words, what to take now and whether I’ve already taken it."'}, {'_map_index': 1, 'name': 'Evelyn Carter', 'segment': 'Elderly users with memory or cognitive decline', 'profile': '# Evelyn Carter\n\n## Demographics\n- **Age:** 78\n- **Location:** Dayton, Ohio, USA\n- **Occupation:** Retired elementary school librarian\n\n## Goals\n1. **Keep her medication schedule straight without relying on memory alone.** Evelyn wants a simple way to see what she needs to take in the morning, after lunch, and at bedtime so she does not have to keep checking pill bottles or a paper list.\n2. **Avoid double-dosing or skipping medications when her routine changes.** If her doctor adjusts a prescription or her daughter fills her pill organizer differently, she wants the app to make the change obvious so she does not accidentally take the wrong pill twice.\n3. **Feel confident that she has taken the right medication at the right time.** She wants clear confirmations and reminders that help her trust her own routine, especially on days when she feels foggy or distracted.\n\n## Frustrations\n1. **Small labels and confusing medication names.** Evelyn gets tripped up by bottles with tiny print, similar-looking pills, and names she cannot easily pronounce, which makes it hard for her to tell one medication from another.\n2. **Too many alerts or unclear reminders.** She becomes anxious when reminders are loud, repetitive, or vague. If an app just says “Take your meds,” she is not sure which one it means or whether she already took it.\n3. **Changes from doctors and pharmacies are hard to track.** When a prescription is refilled with a different-looking pill or a dosage changes, she can lose confidence and worry she is using the wrong instructions.\n\n## Context\n- **Tech comfort level:** Moderate. Evelyn uses a smartphone for calls, photos, and weather, but she is cautious with new apps and often asks her daughter to help set things up. She prefers large buttons, simple screens, and very few steps.\n- **Daily routine:** She wakes around 7:00 a.m., has coffee, watches the news, and takes several medications throughout the day. Her routine is fairly steady, but afternoons can be confusing if she naps or gets interrupted by appointments, visitors, or phone calls.\n- **Environment:** She lives alone in a small ranch house, with her daughter checking in a few times a week. Her kitchen counter is where she keeps pill organizers, glasses, and appointment notes, so she often manages medications in a busy, physical space rather than a quiet one.\n\n## Key quote\n*“I don’t need fancy — I just need something that tells me plainly what to take and when, so I don’t have to second-guess myself.”*'}, {'_map_index': 2, 'name': "Margaret O'Neill", 'segment': 'Seniors living alone without regular caregiver support', 'profile': '### Demographics\n- **Age:** 78\n- **Location:** Leeds, West Yorkshire, UK\n- **Occupation:** Retired primary school librarian\n\n### Goals\n- **Keep her medication schedule straight without relying on memory.** Margaret wants one place that tells her exactly what to take, when to take it, and whether she’s already taken it today.\n- **Avoid accidental double-dosing or missed doses.** She worries most about taking her blood pressure tablets twice or forgetting her evening pill after dinner.\n- **Get clear reminders she can trust.** She wants the app to nudge her in a way that is hard to miss but not alarming, especially on days when her routine changes.\n\n### Frustrations\n- **Pill boxes become confusing when prescriptions change.** When the doctor adjusts a dose or adds a short-term antibiotic, she has to re-check the labels and often loses track of which tablet is which.\n- **Small text and cluttered screens are hard to read.** Margaret gets frustrated by apps that hide the important information behind too many taps, tiny buttons, or medical jargon.\n- **She has no one to easily confirm she’s doing it right.** Because she lives alone and doesn’t have a regular caregiver, she worries there’s nobody to notice if she misses several doses in a row.\n\n### Context\nMargaret is moderately comfortable with technology. She uses a smartphone for calls, WhatsApp, and photos of her grandchildren, but she is cautious about apps that ask for too many permissions or setup steps. She keeps her phone charged in the kitchen and checks it several times a day, usually after breakfast, around lunch, and before bed. Her flat is quiet and tidy, with her medication stored in a labeled basket beside the kettle. She prefers straightforward language, large text, and reminders that fit into her existing routines rather than requiring her to learn a new system.\n\n### Key quote\n> “I don’t need anything fancy — I just want to know, at a glance, whether I’ve taken the right tablet at the right time.”'}, {'_map_index': 3, 'name': 'Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill', 'segment': 'Older adults with complex medication schedules and refill needs', 'profile': '## Demographics\n- **Age:** 76\n- **Location:** St. Paul, Minnesota, USA\n- **Occupation:** Retired elementary school librarian\n\n## Goals\n- **Keep track of a complicated daily medication routine** without having to rely on sticky notes, pillbox labels, or memory alone. She wants a clear, at-a-glance view of what to take, when to take it, and whether it should be taken with food.\n- **Avoid missed refills and last-minute pharmacy trips** by getting early reminders when a prescription is running low, especially for her blood pressure medication and inhaler.\n- **Share medication updates with her daughter** so someone else can quickly see if she skipped a dose, changed a prescription, or needs help arranging a refill.\n\n## Frustrations\n- **Her medications change often** after specialist visits, and she struggles to remember which pill was discontinued, which dosage changed, and which bottle is the “new” one.\n- **Pharmacy refill timing is confusing** because some prescriptions can be refilled early, some require doctor approval, and some arrive by mail, making it hard to know what is actually on the way.\n- **Small text and cluttered screens slow her down**, especially when apps use tiny buttons, medical jargon, or too many steps just to confirm a dose or request a refill.\n\n## Context\n- **Tech comfort level:** Moderate. Maggie uses an iPhone, checks email, video calls her grandchildren, and can manage basic apps, but she gets frustrated if setup takes too long or if an app assumes she already understands medical terms.\n- **Daily routine:** She wakes around 7:00 a.m., makes coffee, and takes her first medications with breakfast. She keeps a paper list in the kitchen, but she often double-checks it against pill bottles before bed. On Tuesdays, she does a “medication audit” to make sure she has enough for the week.\n- **Environment:** Lives alone in a quiet condo. She keeps her phone on the kitchen counter and often uses it with reading glasses. She prefers reminders that are audible and easy to dismiss or snooze without confusion.\n\n## Key quote\n> “I don’t need the app to be fancy — I need it to tell me, plainly, what to take now and when to refill it before I run out.”'}, {'_map_index': 4, 'name': 'Maya Thompson', 'segment': 'caregivers helping an elderly family member manage meds', 'profile': '## Maya Thompson\n\n### Demographics\n- **Age:** 42\n- **Location:** Columbus, Ohio\n- **Occupation:** Payroll coordinator at a mid-sized manufacturing company\n\n### Goals\n1. **Keep her mother’s medication schedule accurate without constant phone calls.** Maya wants a simple way to see what her mom should take, when she took it, and whether anything was missed so she doesn’t have to text her between meetings.\n2. **Get reliable reminders that her mother will actually notice.** She wants the app to send clear, easy-to-follow prompts on her mom’s phone and let Maya know when a dose is skipped or delayed.\n3. **Avoid medication mistakes when prescriptions change.** Maya needs a fast way to update new pills, dosage changes, and refill dates after doctor visits so the app stays trustworthy.\n\n### Frustrations\n1. **Her mother mixes up pill names and dosage times.** Maya is constantly translating instructions from bottles, discharge papers, and pharmacy labels into something her mom can understand.\n2. **She can’t tell if a medication was taken unless she asks.** Right now she relies on memory, sticky notes, and occasional check-ins, which makes it hard to know if a dose was missed.\n3. **Managing changes is messy and stressful.** When a doctor changes a prescription, Maya has to update multiple places and worry that one outdated reminder will cause confusion.\n\n### Context\n- **Tech comfort level:** Moderately comfortable. Maya uses smartphones, calendar apps, and online banking confidently, but she gets impatient with apps that have too many steps or unclear settings.\n- **Daily routine:** She works 8:30–5:00 on weekdays, checks in on her mother before work and again after dinner, and often handles med-related tasks during short breaks or while waiting in pickup lines.\n- **Environment:** Maya is juggling work, two school-age kids, and her mother’s care from her own home. She needs an app that works quickly, with minimal typing, and is easy to use while distracted.\n\n### Key quote\n> “I don’t need another complicated app — I just need to know my mom took the right pills at the right time.”'}]
  all_scenarios: [{'_map_index': 0, 'persona_name': 'Margaret Ellis', 'scenarios': ['# Morning Medication Check\n\n**User Story**: As Margaret Ellis, I want to quickly see my morning medications in plain language so that I can take the right pills after breakfast without relying on memory.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Margaret opens the app at her kitchen table after breakfast.\n2. She sees a large, high-contrast list of medications due now, with each name and dosage written clearly.\n3. She taps the first medication to confirm it matches the bottle on the counter.\n4. After taking the pill, she marks it as taken and sees an immediate confirmation.\n5. She repeats the process for each morning prescription until the app shows that her morning doses are complete.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- One bottle label is hard to read, so she needs the app to show the medication name in a larger font than the bottle.\n- A medication is scheduled for later in the morning, and she needs to avoid accidentally marking it taken too early.\n- She forgets whether she already took one pill, and the app must clearly show its current status to prevent double-dosing.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Margaret feels relieved when the app gives her a simple, trustworthy checklist. She is cautious at first, but becomes more confident once she sees clear confirmation for each dose.\n\n# Lunch-Time Reminder With Clear Action\n\n**User Story**: As Margaret Ellis, I want a noticeable reminder at lunch time that tells me exactly which medication to take so that I do not dismiss the alert and forget the dose.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Margaret is watching the news when the app sends a lunch-time reminder.\n2. The notification uses large text and names the medication in plain words, not just a generic alarm.\n3. She taps the alert to open the app and sees only the medication due now.\n4. She reviews the dosage and takes the pill from her organizer near the coffee maker.\n5. She marks the dose as taken and the app confirms it with a clear message.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- The reminder arrives while the TV is on, so it needs to be noticeable without being startling.\n- Margaret dismisses a vague notification by habit, so the app must make the action obvious before she closes it.\n- She is unsure whether lunch has been delayed, and the app should still show the dose window clearly.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Margaret wants reminders that feel helpful, not nagging. She feels more at ease when the alert is specific and easy to act on immediately.\n\n# Preventing a Double Dose\n\n**User Story**: As Margaret Ellis, I want the app to clearly show when I have already taken a medication so that I do not accidentally take it twice later in the day.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Later in the afternoon, Margaret wonders whether she already took one of her medications.\n2. She opens the app and checks the day’s medication timeline.\n3. The app shows the medication as already taken with a clear timestamp.\n4. She compares the app status with her pill organizer and feels reassured.\n5. She leaves the medication alone and continues her day without guessing.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- She forgot to tap “taken” earlier, so the app needs to help her confirm whether the dose was actually missed or just not recorded.\n- Two medications have similar names, and she needs the app to distinguish them clearly.\n- She is not sure if the timestamp is from today or yesterday, so the app must make the date obvious.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Margaret feels anxious when she cannot remember whether she already took a pill. Clear status information helps her feel safe and in control.\n\n# Refill and Prescription Change Update\n\n**User Story**: As Margaret Ellis, I want to get refill reminders and update medication changes easily so that I can keep my schedule accurate when prescriptions change.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Margaret checks the app and sees that one prescription is running low soon.\n2. The app shows a refill reminder with the medication name and remaining supply in simple terms.\n3. She taps the reminder and reviews the refill details before calling or visiting the pharmacy.\n4. After the doctor changes a dose, she opens the medication entry and updates the new instructions.\n5. The app confirms the updated schedule and shows the next dose time in plain language.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- A doctor temporarily pauses a medication, and Margaret needs the app to make it obvious that it is on hold.\n- The pharmacy switches her to a different strength, and she must avoid confusing the old bottle with the new one.\n- She is unsure how to edit the schedule, so the app needs a very simple path for updating changes.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Margaret feels stressed when routines change because she does not want to make a mistake. She feels reassured when the app helps her keep everything current without a complicated setup.']}, {'_map_index': 0, 'persona_name': 'Evelyn Carter', 'scenarios': ['# Morning Medication Check-In\n\n**User Story**: As Evelyn Carter, I want to see my morning medications in a clear, simple list so that I can take the right pills without having to rely on memory.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Evelyn opens the app after breakfast while sitting at her kitchen counter.\n2. The app shows a large, high-contrast card labeled “Morning meds” with only the medications due now.\n3. She taps each medication name to see a plain-language description and confirms the pill matches what is in her organizer.\n4. After taking them, she taps a big “Taken” button for each item.\n5. The app gives a calm confirmation and shows the next scheduled dose time.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- One medication name looks unfamiliar because the pharmacy changed the generic brand name, making Evelyn hesitate.\n- She is interrupted by a phone call before finishing, and she worries she may have already marked one pill as taken.\n- The screen text is too small or crowded, making it hard for her to tell which pills are morning-only.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Evelyn feels cautious at first, then relieved when the app clearly tells her what to take. She wants reassurance more than speed, and a simple confirmation helps her feel confident she has started the day correctly.', '# After-Lunch Reminder When Routine Is Interrupted\n\n**User Story**: As Evelyn Carter, I want a gentle reminder for my after-lunch medication so that I do not forget it when my afternoon routine is disrupted.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Evelyn finishes lunch and leaves her pill organizer on the counter while she goes to answer the door or rest on the couch.\n2. At the scheduled time, the app sends a quiet reminder with the specific medication name and a large button to open the dose.\n3. She opens the reminder and sees exactly which pill to take, along with a simple note like “Take with food” if needed.\n4. She takes the medication and taps “Taken.”\n5. The app confirms the dose without additional alarms and updates the day’s schedule.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- The reminder arrives while she is napping, and she might miss it if it is too subtle.\n- She cannot remember whether lunch was early or late, so she is unsure if the reminder is for now or later.\n- Too many repeated alerts make her anxious and more likely to ignore the app.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Evelyn feels a little scattered in the afternoon, especially if she has been interrupted. A calm, specific reminder helps reduce worry and gives her confidence that she can stay on track without being overwhelmed.', '# Doctor Changed the Dosage\n\n**User Story**: As Evelyn Carter, I want the app to clearly show when a medication dosage has changed so that I do not accidentally take the old amount.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Evelyn’s daughter updates the app after a doctor appointment or helps her enter the new prescription details.\n2. The app highlights the change in a clear way, showing the old dose and the new dose side by side.\n3. Evelyn reviews the updated medication card and sees the effective date for the new instructions.\n4. She checks the pill bottle or organizer against the app to make sure the new dose matches what she has at home.\n5. The app confirms the update and uses the new dosage in future reminders.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- The pharmacy refills the prescription with pills that look different, and Evelyn worries she received the wrong medicine.\n- She forgets whether the change starts today or next week and needs the app to make that obvious.\n- A small change in dosage is hard for her to notice if the update is not visually prominent.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Evelyn feels uncertain and a bit anxious when a prescription changes because she does not want to make a mistake. Clear before-and-after information helps her feel safe and reduces second-guessing.', '# Bedtime Double-Check\n\n**User Story**: As Evelyn Carter, I want to review my bedtime medications in one place so that I can avoid taking a pill twice or missing it before bed.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Evelyn opens the app after brushing her teeth and before getting ready for bed.\n2. The app shows a bedtime checklist with only the medications still due tonight.\n3. She sees which pills were already marked taken earlier in the day and which ones remain.\n4. If she is unsure, she taps a medication name to compare it with her pill organizer or bottle label.\n5. She marks the remaining medication as taken and sees a clear end-of-day confirmation.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- Evelyn cannot remember whether she already took the pill after dinner and needs the app to make past doses easy to review.\n- Similar-looking evening medications make her worry she may choose the wrong one.\n- If the app uses vague wording like “complete your routine,” she may not know exactly what is left.\n\n**Emotional Context**: At bedtime, Evelyn is often tired and less confident in her memory. She feels reassured when the app acts like a calm checklist, helping her end the day without doubt.']}, {'_map_index': 0, 'persona_name': "Margaret O'Neill", 'scenarios': ["## Title\nMorning check-in before breakfast\n\n## User Story\nAs Margaret O'Neill, I want to see my morning medicines clearly and confirm when I have taken them so that I do not accidentally miss or repeat a dose.\n\n## Steps\n1. Margaret opens the app after putting the kettle on in the kitchen.\n2. She sees a large, simple list of today’s medicines with the morning dose highlighted.\n3. She taps the medication she is about to take to read the name, dose, and any short instructions in plain language.\n4. After taking the tablet, she taps “Mark as taken” to confirm it.\n5. The app updates immediately so she can see at a glance that her morning dose is complete.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- The app shows too much detail or small text, making it hard for Margaret to quickly confirm the right tablet.\n- She is interrupted by a phone call or door knock and forgets whether she already marked the dose as taken.\n- A prescription change means the morning medicine list no longer matches the label on her pill basket, causing uncertainty.\n\n## Emotional Context\nMargaret feels cautious but relieved when the app gives her a clear, trustworthy answer. She wants reassurance without fuss, and she feels more confident when the screen is simple and readable.\n\n## Title\nEvening reminder after dinner routine\n\n## User Story\nAs Margaret O'Neill, I want a clear reminder for my evening tablet so that I remember to take it even when my routine changes.\n\n## Steps\n1. Margaret finishes dinner and glances at her phone on the kitchen counter.\n2. The app sends a gentle but noticeable reminder for her evening medication.\n3. She opens the reminder and sees exactly which tablet to take and whether it should be taken with food or water.\n4. She takes the medicine and marks it as taken.\n5. The app confirms completion and removes the reminder for the rest of the evening.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- The reminder arrives while Margaret is busy or distracted, and she dismisses it without acting on it.\n- A reminder sound is too subtle, so she misses it when the flat is quiet.\n- The instructions are phrased in medical language that she does not easily understand.\n\n## Emotional Context\nMargaret feels slightly anxious about forgetting her evening pill, especially if her routine is disrupted. A calm, dependable reminder helps her feel in control rather than nagged.\n\n## Title\nUpdating a new prescription after a GP change\n\n## User Story\nAs Margaret O'Neill, I want to update my medication list when my GP changes a prescription so that I do not confuse old tablets with new ones.\n\n## Steps\n1. Margaret notices the pharmacy label has changed after a GP appointment.\n2. She opens the app and goes to her medication list.\n3. She edits the existing medicine or adds the new one using simple prompts and large text.\n4. She confirms the new dose, timing, and any short-term instructions.\n5. The app updates her schedule so today’s reminders reflect the new prescription.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- Margaret is unsure whether the new tablet replaces the old one or is taken alongside it.\n- The app asks for too many setup details, making her worry she entered something incorrectly.\n- A short-term antibiotic overlaps with her regular medicines and creates confusion about which dose to follow first.\n\n## Emotional Context\nMargaret feels vulnerable when prescriptions change because this is when mistakes are most likely. She wants the app to feel like a clear second pair of eyes, not another complicated task.\n\n## Title\nChecking whether a dose was already taken\n\n## User Story\nAs Margaret O'Neill, I want to quickly check if I have already taken a medicine so that I do not double-dose when I cannot remember.\n\n## Steps\n1. Margaret looks at her phone later in the day and wonders whether she took her blood pressure tablet.\n2. She opens the app and views today’s medication history.\n3. The app shows a clear taken/not taken status with the time it was marked.\n4. She compares it with her routine and confirms whether the dose is due or already done.\n5. If needed, she marks the dose as taken or leaves it unchanged.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- Margaret cannot remember if she marked the dose before or after an interruption, so the status still feels uncertain.\n- The history view is buried behind too many taps, making it hard to check quickly.\n- A sync delay or app error shows outdated information, which could increase her worry.\n\n## Emotional Context\nMargaret feels uneasy when she cannot trust her memory. A clear history view gives her relief and helps her avoid the fear of taking the wrong tablet twice.\n\n## Title\nHandling a missed dose without support\n\n## User Story\nAs Margaret O'Neill, I want the app to help me respond clearly when I miss a dose so that I know what to do next without needing to ask someone.\n\n## Steps\n1. Margaret notices she missed a reminder while she was out or resting.\n2. She opens the app and sees that the dose is marked as missed.\n3. The app gives a simple next-step message in plain language, such as whether to take it now or wait until the next scheduled dose.\n4. She follows the guidance and confirms the action in the app.\n5. The app updates her schedule and shows the next reminder clearly.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- The guidance is too vague, leaving Margaret unsure whether it is safe to take the medicine late.\n- She misses multiple doses in a row and worries that the app is not warning her strongly enough.\n- The app uses jargon or references she would normally ask a caregiver about, but she has nobody to consult.\n\n## Emotional Context\nMargaret feels worried and a little isolated when she misses a dose. She is reassured by calm, direct guidance that tells her exactly what to do next without making her feel at fault."]}, {'_map_index': 0, 'persona_name': 'Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill', 'scenarios': ['# Title: Morning Medication Check at the Kitchen Counter\n\n**User Story**\nAs Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill, I want to see my morning medications in a simple, readable list so that I can take the right pills with breakfast without second-guessing myself.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Maggie opens the app after making coffee and sees a large, high-contrast “Now” screen with only her current morning doses.\n2. She taps each medication one by one to review whether it should be taken with food and whether it has any special instructions.\n3. She confirms the first dose as taken and the app marks it clearly with a large visual checkmark and spoken confirmation.\n4. She notices one medication is flagged as “changed recently” and taps it to see the updated dosage in plain language.\n5. After finishing breakfast, she dismisses the remaining reminder or snoozes it if she is not ready yet.\n\n**Edge Cases**\n- A medication name looks unfamiliar because her specialist changed the bottle, and she needs the app to show both the old and new names.\n- The screen text is too small or crowded, making it hard for her to confirm the dose confidently.\n- She accidentally taps the wrong medication and needs an easy way to undo the confirmation.\n\n**Emotional Context**\nMaggie feels relieved when the app is simple and reassuring, but anxious if anything looks different from her paper list. She wants confidence, not complexity, first thing in the morning.', '# Title: Tuesday Weekly Medication Audit\n\n**User Story**\nAs Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill, I want to review all my medications and remaining supply each Tuesday so that I can spot low bottles and avoid running out unexpectedly.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Maggie opens the app during her regular Tuesday medication audit and goes to a summary view of all active prescriptions.\n2. She scans the list for each medication’s remaining count, refill date, and whether it is mail-order or pharmacy pickup.\n3. The app highlights one blood pressure medication as running low soon, and she taps it for more details.\n4. She reviews the refill status in plain language and sees whether it can be requested now or needs approval.\n5. She sets a reminder for the next action, such as calling the pharmacy or waiting for delivery.\n\n**Edge Cases**\n- A prescription is marked as refillable, but the pharmacy has not yet updated the app, creating confusion about whether it is actually on the way.\n- A mail-order medication has a different timeline than her local pharmacy prescriptions, and she needs the app to explain the difference clearly.\n- She sees a discontinued medication still listed and worries she may have been taking the wrong bottle.\n\n**Emotional Context**\nMaggie feels practical and focused during her weekly audit, but she can become worried if refill information is vague or contradictory. Clear status updates help her feel in control.', '# Title: Managing a Prescription Change After a Specialist Visit\n\n**User Story**\nAs Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill, I want to understand which medication changed after a doctor visit so that I do not confuse the new prescription with the old one.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Maggie receives a notification that one of her medications has been updated after a specialist appointment.\n2. She opens the app and sees a simple change summary showing what was stopped, what was added, and what dosage changed.\n3. She taps the medication to compare the old and new versions side by side in plain language.\n4. She marks the new bottle as active and the old one as discontinued so the app stops reminding her about the outdated dose.\n5. If needed, she shares the update with her daughter so someone else can verify the change.\n\n**Edge Cases**\n- The app uses medical jargon like “titration” or “PRN,” which confuses her and slows her down.\n- Two bottles look similar, and she needs a photo or clear label to tell which one is the new prescription.\n- Her daughter sees a different version of the medication list and Maggie worries the shared information is not synchronized.\n\n**Emotional Context**\nMaggie feels uncertain after a prescription change because she does not want to mix up old and new instructions. She feels much better when the app explains changes plainly and visually.', '# Title: Refill Reminder Before Running Out\n\n**User Story**\nAs Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill, I want early refill reminders for low medications so that I can arrange pharmacy pickup or delivery before I run out.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Maggie gets an audible reminder that one prescription is getting low, with enough lead time to act before the bottle is empty.\n2. She opens the reminder and sees the medication name, remaining days, and refill method in large text.\n3. The app tells her whether she can request a refill now, needs doctor approval, or should expect mail delivery.\n4. She chooses the appropriate action, such as sending a refill request or setting a reminder to call the pharmacy.\n5. The app confirms the next expected step and adds it to her upcoming tasks.\n\n**Edge Cases**\n- The app warns her too late, and she feels rushed because she may not be able to get to the pharmacy right away.\n- A refill requires approval, but the app does not clearly explain who needs to act next.\n- She accidentally dismisses the reminder and needs an easy way to find it again later.\n\n**Emotional Context**\nMaggie feels reassured when the app gives her time to plan ahead. She feels stressed when refill timing is unclear or when she fears a last-minute trip.', '# Title: Sharing Medication Status with Her Daughter\n\n**User Story**\nAs Margaret “Maggie” O’Neill, I want to share my medication updates with my daughter so that she can help me notice missed doses, changes, or refill needs.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Maggie opens the sharing settings and chooses her daughter as a trusted contact.\n2. She selects what to share, such as missed doses, medication changes, and refill alerts.\n3. The app shows a plain-language preview of what her daughter will see so Maggie can confirm it feels appropriate.\n4. Maggie sends the invitation and sees that sharing is active.\n5. When a dose is missed or a refill is needed, the app automatically updates her daughter with a simple summary.\n\n**Edge Cases**\n- Maggie worries about sharing too much personal information and wants tight control over what her daughter can see.\n- Her daughter is not very tech-savvy either, so the shared view needs to be easy to understand at a glance.\n- A medication change happens, but the update does not reach her daughter right away, causing confusion.\n\n**Emotional Context**\nMaggie feels comforted knowing someone else can help keep an eye on things, but she also wants to stay independent. She needs sharing to feel supportive, not intrusive.']}, {'_map_index': 0, 'persona_name': 'Maya Thompson', 'scenarios': ['## Title: Morning Check-In Without a Phone Call\n\n**User Story**: As Maya Thompson, I want to quickly confirm whether my mother took her morning medications so that I can start my workday without worrying or interrupting her.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Maya opens the app during a quick break before 8:30 a.m.\n2. She sees a simple status summary showing which morning doses are due, taken, or missed.\n3. She taps her mother’s profile to review the last logged dose and any reminder activity.\n4. She sends a gentle reminder to her mother’s phone with one tap if a dose is still pending.\n5. The app updates Maya with a clear confirmation once her mother marks the medication as taken.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- Maya is in a hurry and needs the answer in one screen, but the app buries the status behind multiple menus.\n- Her mother forgot to mark a dose as taken even though she did take it, creating uncertainty for Maya.\n- A reminder fails to reach her mother’s phone because it is off, silenced, or out of battery.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Maya feels rushed and mentally split between work and caregiving. She wants reassurance fast, and she feels relieved when the app gives her a clear answer without needing to text or call.\n\n## Title: Updating a Prescription After a Doctor Visit\n\n**User Story**: As Maya Thompson, I want to update my mother’s medication list right after a doctor changes a prescription so that the reminders stay accurate and we avoid mistakes.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. After a doctor appointment, Maya opens the app while still at the clinic or in the car.\n2. She edits the existing medication or adds a new one using the prescription label details.\n3. She sets the new dosage, timing, and start date with minimal typing.\n4. She reviews the schedule preview to make sure the old reminder is replaced correctly.\n5. She saves the change and confirms the updated instructions are now visible on her mother’s phone.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- The new prescription conflicts with an older reminder, and Maya needs the app to clearly flag the overlap.\n- Maya is using discharge papers and a pharmacy label at once and may accidentally enter the wrong dosage or timing.\n- She is updating while distracted in a parking lot or pickup line and needs a very fast, error-resistant flow.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Maya feels stressed and cautious because medication changes are the moments most likely to cause errors. She wants confidence that the app now matches the doctor’s instructions exactly.\n\n## Title: Catching a Missed Dose After Work\n\n**User Story**: As Maya Thompson, I want to see whether my mother missed or delayed a dose during the day so that I can decide whether to follow up or just adjust the schedule.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Maya checks the app after dinner when she usually reconnects with her mother.\n2. She views the day’s medication timeline and sees which doses were taken on time, delayed, or missed.\n3. She taps the missed dose to see when the reminder was sent and whether it was acknowledged.\n4. She decides whether to call her mother, send a follow-up reminder, or log the dose as taken late.\n5. She reviews the updated record so she knows what happened before the next day starts.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- Her mother took the dose but forgot to open the app, making the record look missed.\n- The app shows a delayed dose, but Maya needs to know whether it is still safe to take based on the schedule.\n- Maya is checking while helping with homework or dinner and needs the information to be readable at a glance.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Maya feels concerned and slightly guilty when she sees a possible missed dose. She wants clarity more than detail, because uncertainty is what creates the most stress.\n\n## Title: Setting Up Clear Reminders Her Mother Will Notice\n\n**User Story**: As Maya Thompson, I want to configure reminders that are simple and noticeable for my mother so that she is more likely to take the right medication on time.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Maya opens reminder settings for her mother’s profile.\n2. She chooses reminder times that match her mother’s routine, such as breakfast and bedtime.\n3. She selects a clear reminder style with large text and a straightforward message.\n4. She adds a backup alert so Maya is notified if the dose is not acknowledged.\n5. She saves the settings and tests the reminder to make sure it is easy for her mother to understand.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- Her mother ignores vague reminders, so the message needs to be specific about which pill to take.\n- Maya accidentally sets reminders too close together, which could confuse her mother and create duplicate alerts.\n- The app allows too much customization, and Maya gets frustrated trying to find the simplest option quickly.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Maya feels practical but impatient. She wants a setup that works in real life, not a complicated system she has to babysit every day.\n\n## Title: Checking Refill Timing Before Meds Run Out\n\n**User Story**: As Maya Thompson, I want to track refill dates for my mother’s medications so that I can avoid last-minute pharmacy runs and missed doses.\n\n**Steps**:\n1. Maya opens the app during a lunch break and reviews the refill status for each medication.\n2. She sees which prescriptions are low, due soon, or already ready for pickup.\n3. She taps a medication to confirm the remaining supply and next refill date.\n4. She sets a reminder for herself to handle the refill before the bottle runs out.\n5. She saves the reminder and returns to work knowing the schedule is covered.\n\n**Edge Cases**:\n- A prescription was changed recently, and the refill estimate is no longer accurate.\n- Maya is managing multiple medications and needs the app to highlight only the urgent refill first.\n- The pharmacy information is outdated, so she may need to update pickup details before relying on the reminder.\n\n**Emotional Context**: Maya feels relieved when she can prevent a crisis before it happens. She is especially appreciative of anything that saves her from scrambling at the last minute.']}]
  output_dir: outputs/persona_scenarios/20260527_061530
